  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $Q$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $R$ is not symmetric since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"",
